+Time alignment removes direction-dependent propagation delays before interpolation and thereby reduces the difficulty caused by rapidly varying phase\cite{R3}. Magnitude-corrected and time-aligned interpolation (MCA) further introduces a magnitude-correction filter derived from a frequency-smoothed representation, so that delay compensation, coarse spectral reconstruction, and phase recovery are already handled by an explicit signal-processing pipeline\cite{R1}. Nevertheless, MCA can still leave structured direction-dependent magnitude errors, especially at contralateral high frequencies and around local spectral peaks and notches.
 
-Motivated by this observation, we propose FiLM-conditioned SIREN with Spectral CNN (FSC). The model comprises three cooperating modules. First, a conditional encoder compresses sparse binaural spatial observations into a subject-level embedding, from which feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM)\cite{R7} generates layer-wise modulation parameters. Second, a sinusoidal representation network (SIREN)\cite{R11} forms a continuous residual field over direction, frequency, and MCA magnitude. Finally, a binaural spectral convolutional neural network jointly processes the full spectra of both ears to refine local cross-frequency structure and binaural relationships. The model predicts only an MCA magnitude residual. Reconstruction reuses the MCA phase and the unpredicted frequency bins, maintaining a clear interface between the signal-processing baseline and the learned component.
